      lead: "A left-wing folk label that, in the hands of a Java-born jazz fan, made some of the finest small-group jazz of the 1940s — including Lester Young’s first sessions as a leader — beginning while the major labels were silenced by the recording ban.",
      story: [
        "Eric Bernay, a New York record-store owner, founded Keynote in 1940 to issue folk and protest songs from the Soviet Union and the Spanish Civil War, followed by anti-war records by American musicians [1]. The jazz came from Harry Lim, born in 1919 in Batavia, Java, to a family in the rubber trade, who had run a jazz radio show and the Batavia Rhythm Club before visiting New York in 1940. War stranded him in the United States, where he wrote criticism and ran jam sessions at the Village Vanguard. In late 1943 he offered Bernay his services as a self-financing producer [2].",
        "Lim recorded Benny Carter, Coleman Hawkins and Lester Young, and he gave several musicians their first chances to record as leaders, among them Young and the pianist Lennie Tristano [2]. The Young quartet of late 1943 was one of those sessions [2][3]. Keynote, like Savoy and Apollo, was one of the small independents that kept recording jazz while the American Federation of Musicians’ ban kept the big companies out of the studio [4]. An unwise investment in a pressing plant in 1947 bankrupted the company in 1948, and Mercury took control; Lim, who had left in 1947, lost the rights to everything he had produced [1][2].",
      ],
      sound: "Lim knew what he wanted and, in Donald Clarke’s phrase, got “good sound, with no gimmicks” [2]. Many of his sessions were issued on twelve-inch 78s, unusual for jazz at the time, which gave soloists more “blowing room” than the standard ten-inch side — several years before the LP [2].",
      significance: "Keynote’s politics were on the left: its first catalog was songs of the Soviet Union and the Spanish Republic, and accounts based on FBI surveillance say that during the war the company employed two Soviet agents, Irving Lerner and Arthur Adams [1]. Its jazz output, which John S. Wilson called “an unusually valid reflection of the jazz spirits of the times,” was largely lost from circulation until the 1986 reissue, when Robert Palmer of the New York Times called it “a much more substantial addition to the treasury of absolutely essential classic jazz performances than one could have expected” [1]. Lim, a producer from colonial Indonesia, never owned what he made [2].",
      records: [
        { title: "Lester Young Quartet (Just You, Just Me; I Never Knew; Afternoon of a Basie-ite; Sometimes I’m Happy)", artist: "Lester Young Quartet", artistIds: ["lester-young"], year: 1943, catalog: "Keynote (12-inch)",
          venue: null, piece: null, note: "Young’s first session as leader of his own small group, recorded in late December 1943 [2][3]." },
        { title: "Lennie Tristano trio sides", artist: "Lennie Tristano with Billy Bauer", artistIds: ["lennie-tristano"], year: 1946, catalog: "Keynote",
          venue: null, piece: null, note: "Piano–guitar counterpoint that reviewers at the time found startlingly original [2][5]." },
        { title: "The Complete Keynote Collection", artist: "Various artists", artistIds: ["lester-young", "coleman-hawkins", "benny-carter"], year: 1986, catalog: "Nippon Phonogram/PolyGram (21 LPs)",
          venue: null, piece: null, note: "The full Keynote jazz archive with 115 previously unissued takes [1]." },
      ],
      artists: ["lester-young", "coleman-hawkins", "benny-carter", "lennie-tristano"],
      image: { src: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/%28Portrait_of_Harry_Lim%2C_Yannich_Bruynoche%2C_and_Joe_Thomas%2C_Pied_Piper%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_between_1946_and_1948%29_%28LOC%29_%285395851140%29.jpg/960px-%28Portrait_of_Harry_Lim%2C_Yannich_Bruynoche%2C_and_Joe_Thomas%2C_Pied_Piper%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_between_1946_and_1948%29_%28LOC%29_%285395851140%29.jpg", page: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Portrait_of_Harry_Lim,_Yannich_Bruynoche,_and_Joe_Thomas,_Pied_Piper,_New_York,_N.Y.,_between_1946_and_1948)_(LOC)_(5395851140).jpg",
        caption: "Keynote’s producer Harry Lim with Yannich Bruynoche and Joe Thomas at the Pied Piper, New York, between 1946 and 1948 (names as given by the Library of Congress).",
        credit: "William P. Gottlieb / Library of Congress", license: "Public domain" },
